BANK.push(
{"id":"i1","d":4,"q":"A global retailer is replacing separate logins for 40 internal web applications with single sign-on. Most applications are modern SaaS services, but several are internally developed and the developers are comfortable with JSON and REST. The architect must choose a federation protocol for the new internal applications that also supports mobile clients. Which is the BEST choice?","o":["OpenID Connect on OAuth 2.0, using the authorization code flow with PKCE","SAML 2.0 with the HTTP POST binding for every application, including the native mobile clients","OAuth 2.0 alone, treating the access token as proof of the user's identity","WS-Federation with the passive requestor profile"],"a":[0],"e":"OIDC adds an identity layer (ID token) to OAuth 2.0, uses JSON/JWT, and works well with mobile and SPAs, especially with PKCE. SAML works for browser SSO but is awkward for native mobile. Plain OAuth is for authorization; using access tokens as identity proof is a known anti-pattern."},
{"id":"i2","d":4,"q":"An application developer wants to use OAuth 2.0 so that a third-party budgeting app can read a customer's transaction history from the bank's API, without the customer giving their bank password to the third party. In OAuth terms, which statement is correct?","o":["Customer = resource owner, app = client, bank identity service = authorization server, bank API = resource server","The bank's API is the client, and the budgeting app is the resource server that holds the data","The customer is the authorization server, and the bank is the resource owner of the data","The budgeting app is the authorization server, because it is the party that requests the token"],"a":[0],"e":"OAuth roles: the resource owner (user) grants the client (third-party app) delegated access, the authorization server issues tokens, and the resource server (API) accepts them. The user never shares credentials with the client."},
{"id":"i3","d":4,"q":"A security architect is reviewing a single-page application that uses the OAuth 2.0 implicit flow to obtain access tokens directly in the browser URL fragment. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Move to the authorization code flow with PKCE","Keep the implicit flow but shorten access token lifetimes to five minutes, so a leaked token is only useful briefly","Switch to the resource owner password credentials grant so that the app collects and handles credentials directly","Keep the implicit flow and add a CAPTCHA to the login page to slow down automated attacks against it"],"a":[0],"e":"The OAuth 2.0 Security Best Current Practice and OAuth 2.1 deprecate the implicit flow because tokens leak via URLs, history, and referrers. Code flow with PKCE is recommended. The password grant is also deprecated because it exposes user credentials to the client."},
{"id":"i4","d":4,"q":"A federal agency is designing a public-facing benefits portal. Applicants must be identity-proofed remotely with high confidence because fraudulent claims are costly. Under NIST SP 800-63, which assurance dimension addresses how confidently the applicant's real-world identity is established?","o":["Identity Assurance Level (IAL)","Authenticator Assurance Level (AAL)","Federation Assurance Level (FAL)","Level of Assurance (LoA) 4 from OMB M-04-04"],"a":[0],"e":"SP 800-63 splits assurance into IAL (identity proofing), AAL (authentication strength), and FAL (federation assertion strength). Identity proofing is IAL. The old single LoA scale was replaced by these three dimensions."},
{"id":"i5","d":4,"q":"A financial institution requires that administrators of its payment systems use authentication that resists phishing, including real-time adversary-in-the-middle proxies. Which authenticator BEST meets this requirement?","o":["FIDO2/WebAuthn security keys or platform authenticators bound to the origin","SMS one-time passcodes sent to each administrator's registered mobile number","TOTP codes from an authenticator app","Push approvals with the device's location shown"],"a":[0],"e":"FIDO2 binds credentials to the website's origin, so a proxy on a different domain can't use them. OTPs and push approvals can be relayed by an AitM proxy, and push is vulnerable to fatigue attacks. SMS is also open to SIM swapping."},
{"id":"i6","d":4,"q":"An organization's identity team wants to automate the creation, update, and removal of user accounts in 25 SaaS applications when employees join, move, or leave. Which standard is BEST suited for this?","o":["SCIM","SAML 2.0, using just-in-time provisioning from assertion attributes","OpenID Connect, using claims in the ID token","LDAP, using directory synchronization agents"],"a":[0],"e":"SCIM is a REST/JSON standard for provisioning and deprovisioning identities across domains. SAML and OIDC handle authentication and SSO, not account lifecycle. LDAP is a directory access protocol and isn't typically exposed by SaaS for provisioning."},
{"id":"i7","d":4,"q":"After an audit, a company finds that 18% of active accounts in its ERP system belong to people who left the company months ago. HR terminations are recorded promptly in the HR system. What is the MOST effective architectural fix?","o":["Make HR authoritative and automate deprovisioning","Ask managers to email IT whenever someone leaves","Run a quarterly report of ERP accounts and disable inactive ones by hand","Set ERP passwords to expire every 30 days, so that leavers' accounts become unusable soon after they leave even without any action"],"a":[0],"e":"Tying the identity lifecycle to the authoritative HR source and automating deprovisioning fixes the root cause. Manual emails and quarterly cleanup leave long exposure windows, and password expiry doesn't remove access, especially with SSO or API tokens."},
{"id":"i8","d":4,"q":"A company is designing role-based access control for 15,000 employees. The initial design produced 9,000 roles, many with only one member. What is the MOST likely cause, and what should the architect do?","o":["Role explosion; consolidate with role mining and use ABAC for contextual attributes","Too few employees for RBAC to work well; switch the ERP to discretionary access control instead","Too many applications in scope; reduce the number of applications the role model covers","Normal RBAC behavior at this scale; no change is needed as long as each role is documented"],"a":[0],"e":"Encoding location, project, and other attributes into roles causes role explosion. Role mining finds common patterns, and hybrid RBAC/ABAC handles dynamic attributes as policy rather than as roles."},
{"id":"i9","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing authorization for a microservices platform. Policies must be written once and enforced consistently by dozens of services in different languages. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Externalize authorization to a central policy engine","Implement checks in each service's own code, following a shared wiki page","Rely on network segmentation between services","Grant all authenticated users full access and log what they do, then review the logs for misuse each month"],"a":[0],"e":"Externalized authorization separates policy from code, gives consistency, and lets policies change without redeploying services. Per-service implementations drift, network segmentation doesn't express user-level authorization, and log-only is not access control."},
{"id":"i10","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing privileged access for cloud administrators. Administrators currently have permanent owner rights across all subscriptions. Which design BEST reduces risk?","o":["Just-in-time, approved, time-limited elevation","Keep permanent owner rights but require longer passwords","Keep permanent rights but review them once a year","Remove every administrator's rights and have the cloud provider's own support staff perform all administrative changes on request"],"a":[0],"e":"JIT removes standing privilege, so a compromised account has no admin rights most of the time. Longer passwords and annual reviews keep standing privilege. Handing everything to the vendor isn't practical and doesn't remove the need for privileged access."},
{"id":"i11","d":4,"q":"An organization uses Kerberos for authentication in its Windows domain. Attackers compromised a domain controller and extracted the KRBTGT account's password hash. Which attack does this enable, and what is the remediation?","o":["Golden tickets; reset KRBTGT twice, appropriately spaced, after evicting the attacker","Pass-the-hash against internal web applications; remediate by enabling TLS on every web server in the domain","Kerberoasting of service accounts; remediate by disabling Kerberos and falling back to NTLM authentication","Silver tickets against one service only; remediate by rebooting the affected server to clear cached tickets"],"a":[0],"e":"With the KRBTGT hash, an attacker can forge TGTs (golden tickets) for any identity. Resetting KRBTGT twice (since both the current and previous passwords are valid) invalidates forged tickets. Silver tickets use a service account's hash, and Kerberoasting cracks service ticket encryption."},
{"id":"i12","d":4,"q":"A security architect is reviewing service accounts in Active Directory. Many have weak passwords and SPNs registered. Which attack is MOST relevant, and what is the BEST mitigation?","o":["Kerberoasting; use gMSAs with AES service tickets","Golden ticket; disable the KRBTGT account","Pass-the-ticket; require smart cards for all service accounts","LDAP injection; enable LDAP signing and channel binding on every domain controller in the forest"],"a":[0],"e":"Any domain user can request service tickets for accounts with SPNs and crack them offline. gMSAs use 120-character random passwords that rotate automatically, making cracking infeasible. Disabling KRBTGT breaks the domain."},
{"id":"i13","d":4,"q":"A company wants to let partners' employees access its supplier portal using their own corporate credentials, without the company managing their passwords. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Federation with the partners' IdPs using SAML or OIDC","Create local accounts for every partner employee and email them their initial passwords","Share a single account per partner company","Let partner employees use social logins such as personal email accounts"],"a":[0],"e":"Federation lets partners manage their own users, so when someone leaves the partner, their access ends. Local accounts create lifecycle problems, shared accounts remove accountability, and personal social logins aren't tied to partner employment."},
{"id":"i14","d":4,"q":"In a SAML 2.0 web SSO flow, which security control on the service provider is MOST important for preventing an attacker from submitting a forged or modified assertion?","o":["Signature validation plus audience, recipient, and time checks","Check that the assertion arrived over HTTPS","Check that the browser has a valid session cookie","Check that the assertion includes an email address"],"a":[0],"e":"Signature validation, carefully implemented to avoid XML signature wrapping, plus audience, recipient, and time checks ensures the assertion is authentic and intended for this SP. HTTPS protects transport but doesn't authenticate the assertion's origin."},
{"id":"i15","d":4,"q":"A company's CIAM (customer identity) platform serves 30 million consumers. Credential stuffing attacks using breached passwords are increasing. Which combination of controls is MOST effective without harming user experience?","o":["Breached-password screening, risk-based adaptive MFA, bot detection, and passkey options","Force every consumer to change their password every 30 days","Require a hardware security key for every consumer account","Lock accounts permanently after three failed login attempts, and require customers to call support to have them unlocked"],"a":[0],"e":"Screening against breached passwords and adaptive MFA target the threat while limiting friction, and passkeys remove passwords entirely. Forced rotation and mandatory hardware keys hurt usability, and permanent lockouts let attackers deny service to real users."},
{"id":"i16","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing access certification (recertification) campaigns. Managers complain they rubber-stamp hundreds of entitlements they don't understand. Which design improvement is MOST effective?","o":["Risk-based campaigns on high-risk entitlements, with plain descriptions and usage data","Increase the review frequency from annual to monthly, so that each review has fewer changes to examine","Make managers certify every entitlement in every application each quarter","Remove access reviews and rely on automated provisioning to keep access correct"],"a":[0],"e":"Rubber-stamping comes from volume and lack of context. Focusing on risk, adding usage data, and plain descriptions make reviews meaningful. More frequent or broader reviews increase fatigue, and provisioning alone doesn't catch privilege creep."},
{"id":"i17","d":4,"q":"An organization is designing identity for workloads (applications and services) in a multicloud environment. Services currently authenticate with long-lived API keys stored in configuration files. Which approach is MOST secure?","o":["Workload identity federation (such as SPIFFE/SPIRE) issuing short-lived, rotated credentials","Encrypted configuration files with the decryption password in an environment variable","Annual manual rotation of the API keys","One API key shared by all services"],"a":[0],"e":"Workload identity gives each service a verifiable identity and short-lived credentials without static secrets. Encrypted files still need a static secret to decrypt, and shared or rarely rotated keys increase blast radius."},
{"id":"i18","d":4,"q":"A security architect is evaluating directory services design. The organization stores sensitive attributes such as national ID numbers in the corporate LDAP directory, where all authenticated users can read all attributes. What is the BEST remediation?","o":["Remove attributes that don't need to be there, and apply attribute-level ACLs to the rest","Encrypt all LDAP connections using LDAPS so that attributes can't be read from network traffic","Move the directory to a cloud-hosted service","Require all users to change their passwords"],"a":[0],"e":"Data minimization plus attribute-level ACLs fixes the exposure. LDAPS protects data in transit but doesn't limit who can read it, and moving to the cloud or changing passwords doesn't address access."},
{"id":"i19","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing access control for a highly sensitive research database. The organization wants to grant access based on project membership, data sensitivity, user clearance, and whether the request comes from a managed device. Which XACML component would retrieve the user's clearance from the HR system during evaluation?","o":["Policy Information Point (PIP)","Policy Enforcement Point (PEP)","Policy Administration Point (PAP)","Policy Decision Point (PDP)"],"a":[0],"e":"The PIP supplies attribute values the PDP needs for a decision. The PEP intercepts requests and enforces decisions, the PAP manages policies, and the PDP evaluates policy."},
{"id":"i20","d":4,"q":"A company wants to remove passwords for its workforce. The architect proposes passkeys. Which statement about passkeys is MOST accurate for enterprise planning?","o":["They're FIDO2 credentials, device-bound or synced; decide whether synced passkeys meet assurance needs for privileged users","They're one-time passwords delivered by SMS to the user's registered phone","They store the user's existing password in the cloud for autofill on other devices","They work only on a single vendor's devices and can't be used across different platforms or browsers"],"a":[0],"e":"Passkeys are FIDO credentials. Synced passkeys improve usability and recovery but depend on the sync provider's account security, so high-assurance roles may need device-bound or attested authenticators. They're cross-platform and contain no password or OTP."},
{"id":"i21","d":4,"q":"A large hospital network gives clinicians shared workstations in patient rooms. Clinicians log in dozens of times per shift and complain that login takes too long, so they leave sessions open and colleagues use them. The CISO wants accountability for every chart access. Which design BEST resolves both concerns?","o":["Tap-in/tap-out proximity badges with SSO and automatic lock when the badge leaves","A 16-character password at every login and a one-minute inactivity timeout on every workstation","One shared account per patient room, with a log of which nurse is on duty","No workstation authentication in clinical areas, since they are physically secured and supervised"],"a":[0],"e":"Tap-in/tap-out badges with SSO make individual authentication fast enough that clinicians stop sharing sessions, giving both usability and accountability. Long passwords and short timeouts make sharing worse, shared accounts lose accountability, and no authentication fails audit requirements."},
{"id":"i22","d":4,"q":"An identity architect is designing token lifetimes for an OIDC-based platform. Access tokens are JWTs validated locally by APIs without contacting the authorization server. A user's access is revoked for misconduct. What is the PRIMARY risk, and how should the design address it?","o":["JWTs stay valid until expiry; use short-lived access tokens and revocable refresh tokens","Revocation takes effect immediately with JWTs, so no design change is needed","JWTs can't be revoked at all, so APIs should switch to static API keys","The ID token may be replayed as an access token by the client application to call the API"],"a":[0],"e":"Locally validated JWTs can't be revoked mid-life without extra checks. Short-lived access tokens (minutes) with revocable refresh tokens limit the window. For high-risk APIs, token introspection or continuous access evaluation (CAEP) can be added."},
{"id":"i23","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing privileged access for database administrators. The DBAs need to run queries on production databases containing personal data, but the organization wants to prevent bulk data extraction. Which combination BEST meets this?","o":["PAM-brokered, recorded sessions with query and database activity monitoring that flags or blocks mass extraction","Read-only DBA accounts with no monitoring","Remove all DBA access to production","Shared administrator accounts to reduce the number of credentials that need to be managed and audited"],"a":[0],"e":"Admins need access, so the design must detect and limit misuse: brokered sessions, recording, and database activity monitoring. Read-only access still allows extraction, removing access stops operations, and shared accounts remove accountability."},
{"id":"i24","d":4,"q":"An organization is defining a joiner-mover-leaver process. Which part of the lifecycle MOST often leads to excessive privileges (privilege creep)?","o":["Movers","Joiners, who start with too few permissions and request extra access that is never removed","Leavers, whose accounts are removed too quickly and later recreated with broader rights","Contractors, who are routinely given administrator rights by default"],"a":[0],"e":"Role changes typically add access without removing old entitlements. Mover workflows must recalculate access from the new role (birthright plus requests) and remove old entitlements, ideally automatically."},
{"id":"i25","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing access for a SaaS application that supports both SAML SSO and local username/password logins. After enabling SSO, the security team finds users can still log in with local passwords that bypass MFA. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Enforce SSO-only sign-in with break-glass accounts","Leave local logins enabled for convenience","Require users to change their local passwords every 30 days","Enable MFA on SSO logins only and accept that local logins bypass it, since few users know they exist"],"a":[0],"e":"Local logins bypass the IdP's MFA and conditional access. Enforcing SSO closes the gap, and break-glass accounts give recovery if the IdP fails, with strong protection and monitoring."},
{"id":"i26","d":4,"q":"A security architect must design break-glass (emergency access) accounts for a cloud tenant. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["A few strongly protected, monitored cloud-only accounts","A single emergency account whose password is shared with all administrators","An emergency account synced from on-premises AD with normal MFA","No emergency accounts, since every administrator already has MFA"],"a":[0],"e":"Break-glass accounts must work when federation, MFA, or conditional access fail. They should be cloud-only, strongly protected, few in number, and monitored so any use triggers an alert. A synced account fails if the directory fails, and a shared password removes accountability."},
{"id":"i27","d":4,"q":"A company implements single sign-on across all applications. What is the MOST significant new risk introduced, and how should it be mitigated?","o":["The IdP becomes a single point of compromise and failure","Users will have more passwords to remember across the applications that still use local logins","Applications will no longer need their own authorization logic","Network bandwidth use will increase significantly"],"a":[0],"e":"SSO concentrates risk: one compromised credential or IdP outage affects every application. Strong authentication, risk-based policies, monitoring, and HA design mitigate this. Applications still need authorization."},
{"id":"i28","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing a token-based authentication system using JWTs. Which validation is MOST important for preventing algorithm confusion attacks?","o":["Allow-list expected algorithms server-side; never let the token's alg header choose validation, and reject 'none'","Check that the token is correctly Base64URL-encoded","Verify that the token contains a username claim","Ensure that the token is less than 8 KB in size, since oversized tokens often indicate tampering"],"a":[0],"e":"Attackers can set alg to 'none' or switch RS256 to HS256 (using the public key as an HMAC secret). Servers must pin expected algorithms and keys. Encoding, usernames, and size checks don't prevent forgery."},
{"id":"i29","d":4,"q":"A company is consolidating three Active Directory forests after acquisitions. Which approach BEST maintains security during the transition?","o":["Selective authentication, SID filtering, and phased migration","Create two-way full trusts between all three forests immediately so that users can access resources everywhere on day one","Disable SID filtering so SID history makes migration easier","Give all administrators Enterprise Admin rights in every forest during the transition"],"a":[0],"e":"Selective authentication limits which accounts can authenticate across the trust, and SID filtering prevents SID history injection. Full trusts and disabled filtering let a compromise in one forest spread to the others."},
{"id":"i30","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing identity for a B2B SaaS platform where each customer organization should manage its own users and roles. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Multi-tenant identity with tenant isolation, delegated tenant admins, and per-tenant IdP federation","A single global directory in which the SaaS vendor manages every customer's users and roles","Separate application instances for each customer, with no shared identity layer","Shared administrator accounts across tenants"],"a":[0],"e":"Delegated tenant administration with isolation and BYO-IdP federation scales and lets customers control their users. Vendor-managed identity doesn't scale, separate instances are costly, and shared admin accounts break tenant isolation."},
{"id":"i31","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing access for a hospital's EHR system. Clinicians must be able to access any patient's record in an emergency, even without a treatment relationship. Which design BEST balances care and privacy?","o":["Break-the-glass access with justification and review","Permanent access to all patient records for all clinicians","Deny access without a treatment relationship under all circumstances","Supervisor approval before any emergency access is granted, recorded in the EHR along with the reason for the request"],"a":[0],"e":"Break-the-glass allows care in emergencies while deterring misuse through justification, alerts, and audit. Permanent broad access invites snooping, strict denial can harm patients, and waiting for approval delays emergency care."},
{"id":"i32","d":4,"q":"An organization uses RADIUS for network device administration. The security architect wants per-command authorization and full accounting of administrator actions on network devices. Which protocol is BETTER suited?","o":["TACACS+","RADIUS with PAP, which encrypts the full packet payload","LDAP, using group membership for command authorization","Kerberos, using service tickets for each device"],"a":[0],"e":"TACACS+ encrypts the full payload, separates AAA functions, and supports command-level authorization and accounting, which makes it the usual choice for device administration. RADIUS combines authentication and authorization and encrypts only the password."},
{"id":"i33","d":4,"q":"A company is designing an identity governance program. Auditors want to see that no user holds a toxic combination of entitlements across different applications (for example, create vendor in ERP and release payment in the banking portal). Which capability is required?","o":["Cross-application SoD rules in the IGA platform","Role-based access control configured within each application separately","Annual manager access reviews of each application","MFA on both the ERP and the banking portal"],"a":[0],"e":"Toxic combinations can span applications, so an IGA platform that sees all entitlements must evaluate SoD rules preventively (at request) and detectively. Per-application RBAC can't see cross-application conflicts."},
{"id":"i34","d":4,"q":"An architect needs to give a mobile app secure access to APIs on behalf of the user. The app can't keep a client secret confidential. Which OAuth 2.0 approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Public client with authorization code + PKCE, tokens in platform secure storage","Embed a client secret in the app binary and use the client credentials grant to obtain tokens for the user","Use the resource owner password credentials grant, with the app collecting the user's bank password","Use the implicit flow, returning tokens directly to the app through a custom URL scheme redirect"],"a":[0],"e":"Native apps are public clients; any embedded secret can be extracted. PKCE protects the code exchange without a secret. Client credentials is for machine-to-machine, and password and implicit grants are deprecated."},
{"id":"i35","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing authentication for a high-risk wire transfer function in an online banking app. The user is already logged in with MFA. Which additional control BEST protects against session hijacking and malware-driven transactions?","o":["Transaction signing on a trusted device","Require the user to re-enter their password before each transfer","Display a CAPTCHA before every transfer","Extend the session timeout so users aren't interrupted by re-authentication prompts during long banking sessions"],"a":[0],"e":"Transaction signing binds authentication to transaction details (what-you-see-is-what-you-sign), defeating man-in-the-browser malware that changes payees. A password can be captured by the same malware, and CAPTCHA doesn't verify intent."},
{"id":"i36","d":4,"q":"An enterprise wants to reduce the number of standing administrator accounts on Windows servers. Local administrator passwords are currently identical on all servers. What is the BEST first step?","o":["Deploy Windows LAPS for unique, random, rotated local admin passwords with controlled retrieval","Change the shared local administrator password on all servers every 90 days, and store it in the team's password vault","Disable the local administrator account with no alternative","Rename the local administrator account on all servers"],"a":[0],"e":"Identical local admin passwords enable lateral movement via pass-the-hash. LAPS gives unique, rotated passwords with controlled retrieval. Rotating a shared password keeps the problem, disabling without an alternative breaks recovery, and renaming is obscurity."},
{"id":"i37","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing identity proofing for remote onboarding of new bank customers. Which approach provides the HIGHEST assurance while still being remote?","o":["Document check with liveness and biometric match","Knowledge-based questions generated from the applicant's credit history","Verification of the applicant's email address only","Self-asserted identity with a selfie upload"],"a":[0],"e":"Remote proofing at higher assurance uses document authentication, liveness, biometric match, and authoritative source checks. NIST SP 800-63 no longer allows KBA for verification because answers are often available to attackers. Email verification and self-assertion give little assurance."},
{"id":"i38","d":4,"q":"An organization's identity provider supports continuous access evaluation. What is the PRIMARY benefit for the security architecture?","o":["Relying parties can react to critical events in near real time instead of waiting for token expiry","Users never have to authenticate again after their first sign-in, because the session is evaluated continuously in the background","Access tokens no longer expire","MFA is no longer needed"],"a":[0],"e":"CAE and CAEP let identity providers signal events to relying parties, which revoke or re-evaluate sessions immediately. It doesn't remove authentication or MFA."},
{"id":"i39","d":4,"q":"A company's architect is evaluating authentication for operational technology HMIs on the plant floor. Operators wear gloves, workstations are shared, and emergencies need fast action. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Badge-based shift logins with local fail-safe emergency access and full audit","Complex passwords changed every 30 days on each HMI","No authentication on any HMI","Fingerprint readers on every HMI, so that operators can authenticate quickly without remembering passwords"],"a":[0],"e":"OT authentication must suit the environment: fast, glove-friendly methods such as badges, with emergency access and audit. Complex passwords cause sharing, no authentication fails accountability, and fingerprints don't work with gloves."},
{"id":"i40","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing a federation between a company and a cloud service provider. The company wants users to be automatically signed out of the cloud service when they sign out of the corporate IdP. Which capability is needed?","o":["Single logout (SAML SLO or OIDC logout) on both the IdP and the SP","Longer session timeouts on the cloud service, so that sessions end around the same time as the IdP session","Shorter password expiry at the IdP","Disabling SSO for the cloud service"],"a":[0],"e":"Single logout propagates logout from the IdP to participating SPs. Without it, SP sessions persist after IdP logout. Session timeout and password policies don't create logout propagation."},
{"id":"i41","d":4,"q":"A university provides email and learning systems to 60,000 students, 8,000 staff, and thousands of alumni and visiting researchers. People frequently hold more than one affiliation at once (for example, a staff member taking a course). The current design creates a separate account for each affiliation. What is the BEST identity architecture?","o":["One identity per person, with affiliations as attributes that drive access","Separate accounts per affiliation so student and staff access never mix, linked only in the HR system for reporting purposes","One generic account per department, shared by people in that department under the control of a department administrator","Accounts for staff only, with students and alumni receiving temporary guest access to each system as needed"],"a":[0],"e":"One person, one identity, multiple attributes avoids duplicate accounts, orphaned access, and confusion about who did what. Access follows affiliations, and when an affiliation ends, only the access it granted is removed. Separate accounts complicate lifecycle and accountability."},
{"id":"i42","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing access for an AI assistant that will act on behalf of employees to read their email, schedule meetings, and query internal systems. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Delegated, user-scoped OAuth tokens with least-privilege scopes, consent, and audit","A service account with global read access to every mailbox and internal system, so the assistant can serve any employee","Each employee's password shared with the assistant so it can sign in as them","Domain administrator rights so the assistant never hits permission errors"],"a":[0],"e":"Delegation with scoped tokens keeps the assistant within the user's own permissions and is auditable. A global service account creates a powerful target and allows confused-deputy problems, sharing passwords breaks authentication, and domain admin is extreme over-privilege."},
{"id":"i43","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing authentication for API-to-API communication between two internal services. Which method provides strong mutual authentication without shared secrets?","o":["Mutual TLS with internal CA certificates per service, rotated automatically","HTTP Basic authentication over TLS, with a long random password per service","A shared API key passed in a request header","IP address allow-listing between the two services' subnets"],"a":[0],"e":"mTLS authenticates both sides with certificates (asymmetric keys, no shared secret) and encrypts traffic. Basic auth and API keys are shared secrets, and IP addresses aren't identity."},
{"id":"i44","d":4,"q":"An organization wants to implement attribute-based access control. Which prerequisite is MOST critical to its success?","o":["Accurate, authoritative, well-governed attributes with clear ownership","A larger number of well-defined roles, so that attributes can be derived from role membership","A new next-generation firewall in front of the applications that will use ABAC","A password manager for all users, so that credentials used in attribute lookups are strong"],"a":[0],"e":"ABAC decisions are only as good as the attributes. Wrong attributes produce wrong decisions. Governance of attribute sources and quality is the foundation."},
{"id":"i45","d":4,"q":"An enterprise has 300 applications, and most still use their own local user stores. The CISO wants to consolidate. Which approach should the architect prioritize FIRST?","o":["Integrate the highest-risk and most widely used applications first","Integrate applications in alphabetical order, so that progress is easy to track and report","Integrate the easiest applications first, whatever their risk, to show quick progress to the CISO","Wait until all 300 applications can be integrated in a single coordinated cutover"],"a":[0],"e":"Risk-based prioritization gives the greatest risk reduction early. Alphabetical order is arbitrary, easy-first may leave critical systems exposed, and big-bang integration delays all benefits."},
{"id":"i46","d":4,"q":"A security architect is reviewing a design where an internal API trusts any request containing an 'X-User-ID' header set by the API gateway. Backend services are reachable directly on the internal network. What is the MOST significant weakness?","o":["Anyone reaching a backend directly can forge the header; verify a signed token or accept only gateway traffic over mTLS","Rename the header to something less predictable","Use a longer user ID format in the header","Base64-encode the header value so it can't be read or modified easily by an attacker on the network"],"a":[0],"e":"Trusting unauthenticated headers is safe only if the backend is reachable solely through the gateway and the gateway strips client-supplied values. Verifying signed tokens or enforcing gateway-only access with mTLS closes the gap. Renaming or encoding is obscurity."},
{"id":"i47","d":4,"q":"An organization is designing a privileged access model following a tiered approach. Which principle is MOST important?","o":["Higher-tier credentials are never exposed on lower-tier systems","Higher-tier administrators should log in to every system regularly so that they can monitor lower tiers","All administrators in each tier should share a single account so that activity is easier to audit","Lower-tier systems should be able to manage higher-tier systems so that help desk staff can fix problems quickly"],"a":[0],"e":"The tiered model prevents credential theft from lower-trust systems from compromising higher tiers. Tier 0 credentials (domain controllers, the IdP) should be used only on Tier 0 assets and privileged workstations."},
{"id":"i48","d":4,"q":"A company wants to use biometrics for physical access to its data center. Which metric BEST indicates the balance between security and usability for the biometric system?","o":["Crossover error rate (equal error rate)","False rejection rate alone, since it shows how often legitimate staff are turned away at the door","Average enrollment time per user","Number of biometric templates the system can store"],"a":[0],"e":"CER compares overall accuracy across systems: lower is better. For high-security sites you may tune toward a lower FAR (more false rejections). FRR alone, enrollment time, and template count don't show the balance."},
{"id":"i49","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing session management for a web application. Which control MOST effectively prevents session fixation attacks?","o":["Regenerate the session ID after authentication and any privilege change","Use longer session timeouts so that users log in less often and attackers have fewer chances to plant an ID","Pass the session ID in the URL instead of a cookie","Keep the same session ID for the life of the account"],"a":[0],"e":"Session fixation works by planting a known session ID before login. Regenerating the ID at login invalidates the planted ID. Session IDs in URLs make fixation easier, and long-lived IDs increase hijack exposure."},
{"id":"i50","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing identity for contractors who need access for 3-6 months. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Sponsored identities with mandatory end dates, auto-expiry, and sponsor re-attestation","Accounts with no expiry date that are disabled manually by IT when the project manager reports that the contract has ended","Shared contractor accounts per vendor","Personal email addresses as usernames"],"a":[0],"e":"Sponsorship gives accountability, and end dates with auto-expiry prevent orphaned accounts. Manual disablement is often forgotten, shared accounts break accountability, and personal emails aren't tied to the vendor relationship."},
{"id":"i51","d":4,"q":"A company uses an on-premises AD with a cloud IdP synchronized through a sync tool. An attacker gains control of the sync server. What is the MOST likely impact?","o":["Possible credential theft or identity changes in both environments; treat the sync server as Tier 0","Only email delivery between the on-premises and cloud environments will be affected","The attacker will only gain access to the sync server itself, since its service account has limited rights","There is no impact, because directory synchronization only flows one way from on-premises to cloud"],"a":[0],"e":"Identity sync servers hold highly privileged credentials for both directories and may process password hashes. Compromise can lead to full identity takeover, so they need Tier 0 protection."},
{"id":"i52","d":4,"q":"An organization's architect is defining password policies for systems that can't support MFA. Which policy BEST aligns with current NIST SP 800-63B guidance?","o":["15-character minimum for single-factor use, breach screening, and no composition rules or forced rotation","8-character minimum with mandatory upper, lower, number, and symbol, rotated every 60 days","6-character minimum, rotated every 30 days","Passwords generated by IT and changed weekly"],"a":[0],"e":"NIST 800-63B rev 4 sets 15 characters as the minimum when a password is the only factor (8 when used with MFA), recommends breach screening, and prohibits composition rules and periodic forced changes. Complexity and rotation push users toward predictable patterns."},
{"id":"i53","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing access to a sensitive data lake. Data scientists need to query anonymized data, while a small team needs identified data. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Tiered de-identified and identified zones","A single zone with all identified data, available to every data scientist","Copies of identified data on each data scientist's laptop for faster analysis","Broad access for everyone, relying on confidentiality agreements"],"a":[0],"e":"Separating de-identified and identified data with tiered access applies least privilege and purpose limitation. Broad access to identified data, copies on laptops, and reliance on agreements all increase exposure."},
{"id":"i54","d":4,"q":"A company runs a customer-facing application where users log in with social identity providers. The architect is concerned about account takeover when a user's social account is compromised. Which control is MOST helpful?","o":["Risk-based step-up for sensitive actions and anomaly monitoring","Remove social login entirely and require every customer to create a new username and password with the company","Require the social password again every hour","Link each customer to several social accounts"],"a":[0],"e":"Relying entirely on a third-party IdP inherits its risks. Step-up authentication for sensitive actions and anomaly detection add protection under the application's control. Removing social login may hurt adoption, and hourly re-entry ruins usability."},
{"id":"i55","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing an identity architecture where privileged role assignments require approval by two people. Which principle does this enforce?","o":["Dual control","Least common mechanism","Open design","Economy of mechanism"],"a":[0],"e":"Requiring two people to approve privileged grants is dual control, a form of separation of duties that prevents one insider from granting themselves power."},
{"id":"i56","d":4,"q":"A company wants to grant third-party auditors read-only access to specific cloud resources for two weeks. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["A time-bound, resource-scoped read-only role assumed through federation, with expiry and logging","An existing administrator's credentials, shared with the auditors for two weeks and then changed","Permanent IAM users with full read access to the whole account","Exported data emailed to the auditors"],"a":[0],"e":"Federated, time-bound, scoped access with logging gives auditors what they need. Sharing admin credentials and permanent broad users increase risk, and emailing data loses control."},
{"id":"i57","d":4,"q":"An identity architect is designing MFA for the workforce. Users report frequent MFA push notifications they didn't initiate. What is likely happening, and what is the BEST response?","o":["MFA fatigue; enforce number matching or phishing-resistant MFA, and reset the passwords","Normal behavior of push-based MFA when users have several devices; no action is required","A bug in the MFA app; have users reinstall it","Users are signed in on too many devices; limit each user to one device"],"a":[0],"e":"Unexpected pushes mean an attacker has the password and is spamming approvals. Number matching and phishing-resistant MFA block this, and passwords must be reset because they're compromised."},
{"id":"i58","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing an access model for a retail company's store systems. Staff rotate between stores frequently. Which model BEST handles this?","o":["RBAC for job function plus location attributes (ABAC)","A separate role for every combination of store and job, assigned and removed by the store manager","Access to every store for all staff","Shared accounts for each store"],"a":[0],"e":"Hybrid RBAC/ABAC uses roles for function and attributes for context such as location, avoiding role explosion. Per-store roles multiply quickly, global access breaks least privilege, and shared accounts remove accountability."},
{"id":"i59","d":4,"q":"A company's IdP issues SAML assertions to dozens of SPs. The IdP's signing certificate expires in 30 days. What should the architect ensure?","o":["A planned rollover: publish the new certificate early, trust both during transition, then switch","Let the certificate expire and fix each service provider that breaks afterward","Issue the new certificate and switch signing immediately, without notifying the service providers","Disable assertion signing temporarily until every service provider has imported the new certificate"],"a":[0],"e":"Certificate rollover must be coordinated. Publishing the new certificate early and supporting both during transition avoids outages. Letting it expire or switching abruptly breaks SSO, and disabling signing removes integrity."},
{"id":"i60","d":4,"q":"An architect wants to apply least privilege to cloud IAM policies that have grown over time. Which approach is MOST effective?","o":["Right-size policies from actual usage in access logs, with guardrails against wildcard grants","Grant developers administrator rights and monitor for misuse","Remove all permissions and wait for complaints","Copy IAM policies from another organization's published examples, adjusting the account IDs and resource names to match"],"a":[0],"e":"Usage-based right-sizing removes unused permissions with evidence, and guardrails prevent regression. Broad admin rights and 'remove all' are extremes, and copied policies don't fit your environment."},
{"id":"i61","d":4,"q":"A government contractor must comply with a requirement that users authenticate to classified-adjacent systems using a credential that is tied to a vetted identity and stored on tamper-resistant hardware. The workforce already carries a smart card badge for building access. Which approach BEST meets the requirement while reusing existing investment?","o":["Use the PIV/CAC smart card's certificates for logon, with PIN and revocation checking","Print a one-time password on the back of each badge and replace the badges every year","Use the badge's proximity serial number as the username, with a short PIN","Issue software certificates to laptops and keep the badge for doors only"],"a":[0],"e":"PIV-style cards carry PKI credentials on tamper-resistant chips, bound to vetted identities, and need a PIN (two factors). Validating revocation status ensures terminated users lose access. Proximity serials are easily cloned, and software certificates don't meet the hardware requirement."},
{"id":"i62","d":4,"q":"An organization runs an internal certificate-based authentication system. When an employee is terminated, IT revokes the certificate, but some systems continue to accept it for days. What is the MOST likely architectural cause?","o":["Relying systems cache CRLs too long or skip revocation checks","The certificate's key length was too short to be revoked reliably by the certificate authority","The CA signed the certificates with SHA-256 rather than SHA-1","The employee's smart card was not physically collected when they left"],"a":[0],"e":"Revocation only works if relying parties check it promptly. Long CRL caching or disabled checks cause delays. Key length and hash algorithm don't affect revocation, and collecting the card helps but doesn't fix systems that ignore revocation."},
{"id":"i63","d":4,"q":"An organization's helpdesk resets passwords and MFA for callers who answer two security questions. Attackers recently took over several executive accounts this way. What is the BEST redesign?","o":["Strong identity verification for resets","Add a third security question","Accept calls only from the registered number, verified by caller ID","Reset passwords by phone, but never MFA"],"a":[0],"e":"Helpdesk social engineering is a major attack path (Scattered Spider-style). Resets must be as strong as the authenticators they replace. Security questions and caller ID are easily defeated, and password-only reset still lets the attacker in if MFA can then be re-enrolled."},
{"id":"i64","d":4,"q":"A company has implemented SSO through a cloud IdP. The architect wants to restrict access to the finance application only from managed devices in approved countries, while allowing email from anywhere with MFA. Which capability provides this?","o":["Per-application conditional access","A single global MFA requirement applied to every application, with trusted locations exempted","Network firewall rules on the finance application","A separate IdP for the finance application"],"a":[0],"e":"Conditional access lets policies vary by application and context. A global MFA rule can't differentiate, firewalls can't see device compliance, and a separate IdP adds complexity and fragmentation."},
{"id":"i65","d":4,"q":"A security architect is evaluating risks with OAuth consent. Users in the organization have granted third-party apps broad permissions to read their mail and files. What is the BEST control?","o":["Limit user consent to verified publishers and low-risk scopes, require admin approval for risky ones, and review grants","Allow users to consent to any app, since it's their own data","Block every third-party app, including approved tools","Require monthly password changes for everyone, which also revokes any OAuth grants the user has made to third-party applications"],"a":[0],"e":"Consent phishing gets malicious apps durable access that survives password changes. Admin consent workflows for risky scopes and grant reviews control this. Unrestricted consent is risky, blocking everything harms business, and password changes don't revoke OAuth grants."},
{"id":"i66","d":4,"q":"A company is designing an identity architecture for 100,000 IoT devices that will authenticate to a cloud platform. Which approach scales BEST securely?","o":["Per-device X.509 certificates via automated enrollment, with a registry and revocation","A single username and password configured identically on all 100,000 devices at the factory","Device MAC addresses used as credentials","A single API key embedded in the firmware"],"a":[0],"e":"Per-device certificates allow unique identity, revocation, and mutual TLS at scale. Shared credentials and embedded keys mean one compromise affects all devices, and MAC addresses are spoofable."},
{"id":"i67","d":4,"q":"An organization's security architect is designing a 'least privilege' strategy for developers in production. Developers occasionally need production access to troubleshoot incidents. Which design is BEST?","o":["No standing access; time-bound elevation tied to an incident ticket, with recording and expiry","Permanent production access for all developers so incidents can be fixed as quickly as possible","No production access ever; operations does all troubleshooting","Shared production credentials in a team wiki"],"a":[0],"e":"Just-in-time access tied to incidents gives developers access when needed with oversight. Permanent access violates least privilege, a total ban may slow incident resolution, and shared credentials in a wiki are a serious exposure."},
{"id":"i68","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing role engineering for a new ERP system. Which approach is MOST effective in producing a sustainable role model?","o":["Combine top-down business analysis with bottom-up role mining, validated by owners","Copy the role model from the ERP vendor's default configuration and adjust it only when users complain about missing access","Create one role per user","Let each department define its own roles"],"a":[0],"e":"Hybrid role engineering aligns roles with business functions while reflecting how access is really used, and business validation ensures ownership. Vendor defaults rarely fit, per-user roles defeat RBAC, and independent departments create inconsistency."},
{"id":"i69","d":4,"q":"A company wants its partners to access a shared portal. Some partners have their own IdP; small partners do not. Which design BEST accommodates both?","o":["Federation for partners with IdPs, and sponsored guest identities with MFA for the rest","Require every partner, however small, to purchase and run its own identity provider before it can access the portal","Local accounts for all partners, whether or not they have an IdP","A shared account for each small partner"],"a":[0],"e":"A hybrid approach serves both groups securely: federation where possible, managed guest identities with strong lifecycle where not. Forcing IdP purchase isn't practical, and shared accounts remove accountability."},
{"id":"i70","d":4,"q":"An organization stores user credentials for legacy apps in a password vault and injects them during SSO (password vaulting SSO). What is the MAIN limitation compared with federated SSO?","o":["Applications still rely on passwords that can be phished, reused, or stolen","Password vaulting can't be used with web applications at all","Users must remember more passwords than before","MFA can no longer be enforced at the identity provider or vault when password vaulting is used for an application"],"a":[0],"e":"Vaulting improves usability but keeps passwords at the application, which can be attacked directly. Federation removes application passwords. MFA can still be applied at the vault or IdP."},
{"id":"i71","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing authorization for a document service. Users should access documents if they are the owner, a member of a team the document is shared with, or a member of a parent organization with inherited rights. Which authorization model fits BEST?","o":["Relationship-based access control (ReBAC)","Mandatory access control with security labels on each document","Simple RBAC with one role per document","IP-based access control lists on the service"],"a":[0],"e":"ReBAC naturally models ownership, sharing, and inheritance as graph relationships (as used by Google Docs with Zanzibar). MAC is too rigid, per-document roles explode, and IP ACLs don't relate to users."},
{"id":"i72","d":4,"q":"An organization finds that a former administrator's API token still works six months after they left. The token was created for an automation script. What is the BEST long-term control?","o":["Give non-human identities owners and maximum lifetimes, and include them in lifecycle and access reviews","Delete that one token and take no further action","Ask administrators to delete their own tokens when they leave the company, as part of the exit checklist","Disable all APIs that accept tokens"],"a":[0],"e":"Non-human identities and tokens often escape lifecycle management. Ownership, expiry, and inclusion in governance prevent orphaned credentials. One-off deletion and relying on leavers to clean up don't fix the process."},
{"id":"i73","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing an identity solution for a hospital where nurses need temporary access to patients in other wards when covering shifts. Which mechanism BEST supports this?","o":["Time-bound, schedule-driven access that expires automatically","Permanent access to every ward for all nurses, so that coverage never depends on IT making changes in time","Shared ward accounts","Helpdesk tickets for each shift change that are never revoked"],"a":[0],"e":"Linking access to the schedule grants coverage access automatically and removes it after the shift. Permanent broad access and shared accounts violate least privilege, and manual unrevoked grants cause privilege creep."},
{"id":"i74","d":4,"q":"A security architect is designing access controls for a highly sensitive vault of encryption keys. Which control BEST prevents a single administrator from extracting a master key?","o":["M-of-N quorum authorization enforced by the HSM","A single administrator with a very long and complex password, changed every 30 days and stored in a sealed envelope","A firewall in front of the key vault","Encryption of the key vault's backups"],"a":[0],"e":"Quorum controls require multiple custodians for sensitive operations, enforcing dual control in hardware. A strong password or a firewall doesn't stop a single insider, and backup encryption protects a different asset."},
{"id":"i75","d":4,"q":"An organization uses Kerberos. Users authenticate once and access many services without re-entering credentials. Which component issues the service tickets?","o":["The Ticket Granting Service (TGS) on the KDC","The Authentication Service, which issues service tickets directly at login along with the TGT","The application server, which issues its own tickets","The client workstation, which issues tickets to itself"],"a":[0],"e":"In Kerberos, the AS issues the TGT after initial authentication, and the TGS (also on the KDC) issues service tickets in exchange for the TGT. Application servers validate service tickets; they don't issue them."},
{"id":"i76","d":4,"q":"A company wants to reduce risk from compromised OAuth refresh tokens in its single-page application. Which technique is MOST effective?","o":["Refresh token rotation with reuse detection, plus sender-constrained tokens where possible","One-year refresh token lifetimes to reduce prompts","Store refresh tokens in local storage for convenience","Disable refresh tokens and issue long-lived access tokens instead, so that there is no refresh token to steal"],"a":[0],"e":"Rotation with reuse detection exposes stolen tokens, and sender-constrained tokens (DPoP, mTLS) bind tokens to a key held by the client. Long-lived tokens and local storage increase theft impact."},
{"id":"i77","d":4,"q":"A security architect is evaluating risk-based authentication. Which set of signals is MOST useful for detecting suspicious logins?","o":["Device, network, location, and behavioral signals","The user's job title and department, taken from the HR system","The date the user's account was created","The user's browser theme and display language settings"],"a":[0],"e":"Risk engines combine device, network, location, behavior, and credential intelligence to score logins. Job title and account age provide little signal on their own."},
{"id":"i78","d":4,"q":"An organization stores sensitive data in a database accessed by a web application through a single high-privilege service account. Auditors want per-user accountability for data access. Which design BEST provides it?","o":["Propagate end-user identity to the data layer for per-user authorization and audit","Give every user a direct database account and remove the application tier so that the database logs each user's queries","Rename the service account to include 'audit'","Log only the service account's queries"],"a":[0],"e":"Identity propagation preserves 'who' through the tiers, enabling per-user authorization and auditing. Direct database accounts for every user are impractical and bypass application logic, and service-account-only logs lose individual accountability."},
{"id":"i79","d":4,"q":"A company's identity architect is designing account recovery for a consumer app that uses passkeys. Users who lose all devices can't sign in. Which recovery design BEST balances security and usability?","o":["Backup authenticators, plus slow, strong verification for full loss","Allow recovery with just a link sent to the user's registered email address","Allow recovery by answering the security questions chosen at signup","Allow no recovery at all; users who lose every device must create a new account"],"a":[0],"e":"Account recovery is often the weakest link. Multiple authenticators prevent most lockouts, and strong verification with delays and notifications protects full-loss recovery. Email links and security questions are easily abused."},
{"id":"i80","d":4,"q":"An architect is designing identity for a merger where both companies use different IdPs. Employees must access applications in both companies immediately after the merger closes. Which approach provides the FASTEST secure access?","o":["Federation trust between the two IdPs, with attribute mapping and conditional access","Duplicate accounts for every employee in the other company's directory, created by a script from the HR export","Shared administrator credentials between the two IT teams","Merge both directories into one on day one"],"a":[0],"e":"Cross-IdP federation gives quick, controlled access with each company keeping control of its identities. Duplicate accounts create lifecycle risks, shared credentials remove accountability, and day-one directory merges are complex and risky."}
);
